---
import AdSlot from '../components/AdSlot.astro';
import Breadcrumbs from '../components/Breadcrumbs.astro';
import ClassroomBar from '../components/ClassroomBar.astro';
import Faq from '../components/Faq.astro';
import {faqs} from '../lib/faqs.mjs';
import {activitiesForTool} from '../lib/activities.mjs';
import {SPECIAL_ANGLES} from '../lib/diagrams.mjs';
import {exactTrig,radiansLabel} from '../lib/math.mjs';
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';import UnitCircle from '../components/UnitCircle.astro';
const quadrant=a=>a%90===0?'Axis':['I','II','III','IV'][Math.floor(a/90)];
// The table adds 360° (one full turn) after the 16 angles drawn on the chart.
const tableAngles=[...SPECIAL_ANGLES,360];
---
<Layout title="Unit Circle With Tangent: Complete Labeled Chart of Sin, Cos and Tan" headline="Unit circle with tangent: labeled chart and interactive circle" section="Unit circle" description="Complete labeled unit circle with tangent: sin, cos and tan for every standard angle in degrees and radians. Printable chart, table and interactive circle."><Breadcrumbs items={[['Tools','/tools/'],['Unit circle','/unit-circle/']]}/><div class="intro"><div><div class="eyebrow">Trigonometry · chart, table and interactive circle</div><h1>Unit circle with tangent: complete labeled chart of sin, cos and tan</h1><p>The chart labels all 16 standard angles in degrees and radians with their point (cos θ, sin θ) and with tan θ = sin θ / cos θ. Below it are the same values as a table, and an interactive unit circle you can drag.</p></div></div><ClassroomBar activities={activitiesForTool('unit-circle')}/>
<section class="explanation" id="unit-circle-chart"><h2>Labeled unit circle chart with sine, cosine & tangent</h2><p>Every standard angle from 0° to 330°, in degrees and radians, with its point (cos θ, sin θ) and its tangent. Tangent is undefined at 90° and 270°, where cos θ = 0.</p><figure style="margin:0"><a href="/unit-circle-chart.svg" aria-label="Open the unit circle chart at full size"><img src="/unit-circle-chart.svg" width="900" height="900" alt="Complete unit circle with tangent: 16 standard angles from 0° to 330°, each labeled in degrees and radians with its coordinates (cos θ, sin θ) and its tan θ value; tan is undefined at 90° and 270°" style="width:100%;max-width:720px;height:auto;display:block;margin:0 auto"/></a><figcaption class="small-note" style="text-align:center">Unit circle with tangent. Each point is labeled with its angle in degrees and radians, its coordinates (cos θ, sin θ), and tan θ. Select the chart to open it at full size. <a class="text-link" href="/unit-circle-chart.svg" download="unit-circle-chart.svg">Download the printable unit circle chart (SVG) ↗</a></figcaption></figure>
<h3 id="unit-circle-table">Unit circle table: sin, cos and tan for every standard angle</h3><p>The same exact values as text. As decimals, √2/2 ≈ 0.707, √3/2 ≈ 0.866, √3/3 ≈ 0.577, and √3 ≈ 1.732.</p><div class="fact-table-wrap"><table class="fact-table chart-table"><caption class="small-note" style="caption-side:top;text-align:left">Exact values of sine, cosine, and tangent on the unit circle</caption><thead><tr><th scope="col">Degrees</th><th scope="col">Radians</th><th scope="col">sin θ</th><th scope="col">cos θ</th><th scope="col">tan θ</th><th scope="col">Quadrant</th></tr></thead><tbody>{tableAngles.map(a=><tr><td>{a}°</td><td>{radiansLabel(a)}</td><td>{exactTrig(a,'sin')}</td><td>{exactTrig(a,'cos')}</td><td>{exactTrig(a,'tan')}</td><td>{quadrant(a)}</td></tr>)}</tbody></table></div>
<h3>How to remember the chart</h3><ul><li>In quadrant I, the sines of 30°, 45°, and 60° are √1/2 (= ½), √2/2, and √3/2; the cosines run the same list backwards.</li><li>Tangent is sine divided by cosine, so tan 30° = √3/3, tan 45° = 1, and tan 60° = √3. It is 0 wherever sin θ = 0 and undefined wherever cos θ = 0.</li><li>Signs follow the quadrant: “All Students Take Calculus” means all three ratios are positive in I, only sine in II, only tangent in III, and only cosine in IV.</li><li>Each angle in quadrants II–IV reuses a quadrant-I value: 150° has the same reference angle, 30°, as 210° and 330°.</li></ul></section>
<AdSlot type="rectangle"/>
<div class="section-heading no-print" id="interactive-unit-circle"><div><div class="eyebrow">Try any angle</div><h2>Interactive unit circle</h2><p>Drag the point or type an angle from 0° to 360° to see sin θ, cos θ, and tan θ change together.</p></div></div><UnitCircle/>
<div class="related"><a href="/learn/radians-and-degrees/">Radians to degrees chart ↗</a><a href="/learn/trigonometric-ratios/">Sine, cosine & tangent ↗</a><a href="/tools/trig-graphs/">Sine & cosine graphs ↗</a><a href="/practice/">Practice special angles ↗</a></div><AdSlot type="native"/><Faq items={faqs['unit-circle']} title="Unit circle questions"/></Layout>
